[38;5;231m⏺[39m Read NOTES.md, the page files, and all four guideline files (01–04).

  This looks [1mbounded[0m: the change is to an existing page ([38;5;153mpublic/activity.html[39m + [38;5;153mactivity.js[39m), and the data is already
  fully loaded on the client (at most 500 events, per Part 1 decision 2). So I'll ask a few questions, then give you a
  short design here in chat instead of writing a spec. Say so if you'd rather treat it as bigger.

  Some constraints that shape the design:
  - [1mFilter options:[0m the nine event types are fixed (decision 4). Security asked for failed sign-ins to be easy to find
    (decision 7), and the only support tickets so far were people looking for an unrecognized failed sign-in.
  - [1mWorks without JavaScript:[0m filters can be a JS-only extra, but the table content can't be. Right now the table is
    built entirely by JS, so with JS off the page is already empty. I'll come back to this as a separate scope question.
  - [1mNo token sheet:[0m guideline 01 says pages use CSS custom properties from a shared token sheet, but there isn't one in
    this repo, and [38;5;153mactivity.css[39m already uses raw hex and px values. Any filter styling runs into that. Also a scope
    question for later.

  [1mFirst question: what should people be able to filter by?[0m

  - [1mA. Event type only.[0m One control listing the nine types, plus a quick way to show only failed sign-ins. Smallest
    diff, and it covers the support-ticket case directly.
  - [1mB. Event type + date range.[0m Adds start/end date pickers (guideline 03: two date pickers, start before end, a presets
    Select). Adds validation, the [38;5;153mdate_order[39m message, and time-zone edge cases at day boundaries.
  - [1mC. Event type + text search.[0m One search box matching device, location, and details (for example "Firefox" or
    "Madrid"). Good for "was that me?" questions without date handling.
  - [1mD. All three.[0m Probably over the 300-line limit, so likely two commits or two PRs.

  My recommendation is [1mA[0m, possibly with C. The real problem is finding a specific kind of event. Over 90 days, newest
  first, date ordering already does most of what a date filter would. Which do you want?

